## 🔍 Observations
- Local network had very few active hosts; most addresses in `192.168.1.0/24` returned `[host down]`.
- Open HTTP/HTTPS ports (80/443) found on router and IoT-like devices (192.168.1.2, 192.168.1.150) — likely a camera/media device given port **554 (RTSP)**.
- An unusual **port 45485** open on `192.168.1.5` — worth further investigation as it's a non-standard high port.
- Wireshark confirmed all sampled traffic was encrypted (TLS 1.2/1.3), so payload content itself wasn't exposed, but metadata (IPs, ports, timing) was fully visible.

## ⚠️ Potential Security Risks Identified
- Open RTSP (554) suggests a camera/streaming device — if unauthenticated, this could leak live video feeds.
- Unknown high port (45485) open on a host could indicate a custom service, backdoor, or misconfigured application — should be identified and closed/firewalled if unnecessary.
- Router admin interface (443 on gateway) should be checked for default credentials.

## ✅ Recommendations
- Close/firewall unused ports (especially the unidentified 45485).
- Restrict RTSP camera streams behind authentication and VPN access only.
- Change default admin credentials on router/IoT devices.
- Regularly re-scan the network to catch newly exposed services.

